    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2048129989124460985",
      "url": "https://x.com/WWIIMemorial/status/2048129989124460985",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/WWIIMemorial/status/2048129989124460985",
      "text": "U.S. Army soldier David M. Gonzales of Pacoima, California, was posthumously awarded the Medal of Honor for his heroic actions in the Philippines on April 25, 1945.\n\nGonzales joined the Army in early 1944 and, by April 25, 1945, was serving as a Private First Class in Company A, 127th Infantry, 32d Infantry Division on Luzon island in the Philippines. \n\nOn April 25, 1945, Gonzales' company was engaged in combat against Japanese forces at Villa Verde Trail on Luzon island in the Philippines. The Army Air Corps was using a tactic called \"skip bombing\" and were fusing the bombs for delayed action detonation to destroy the labyrinth of Japanese caves and tunnels in the northern Luzon campaign. This fusing allowed the bomb to bury itself deep into the ground before detonation. This event trapped five American soldiers in their standing foxholes. In the face of enemy machine gun fire, Gonzales was digging out fellow soldiers who had been buried in a bomb explosion. Gonzales succeeded in extricating three of the soldiers.  As he completed the liberation of the third, he was hit and mortally wounded, but the comrades for whom he so gallantly gave his life were safely evacuated. \n\nGonzales was 21 years old when he died.\n\n#WeRememberThem",
